 recent album Scrapyard... Quadeca has made leaps and bounds... The first thing that stood out to me about this album is how many influences Quadeca brings into this album, and yet he still manages to make it sound entirely cohesive. Putting this album into a singular genre would be impossible... From glitch pop beats, to trap flows, to Bright Eyes vocal styling, to Beatles-esque delivery, Quadeca traverses these genres flawlessly." This page identifies "Scrapyard" as his new album and states it "traverses these genres flawlessly" (musical influences/genres). It also mentions "it feels like getting wrapped in a blanket of emotions." This is very promising. It mentions the album traverses "genres" (one "something") and evokes a "blanket of emotions" (another "something").

*   ** The Strange Wonders of Quadeca’s “SCRAPYARD” - Campus Times:** "The lyrics are strong throughout “SCRAPYARD” as well. Lasky laments common Gen Z anxieties with self-awareness and a knack for metaphor. Many of the tracks seem to deal with feelings of heartbreak as well, and Lasky manages to stray away from cliches while still getting his message across." This article identifies "Scrapyard" as the new album and highlights "common Gen Z anxieties" and "feelings of heartbreak" as lyrical themes. These could be the "somethings" the journey traverses.

*   ** Quadeca's SCRAPYARD: A Colourful Collage of Genre-Bending Experiments:** "There is also the song 'WAY TOO MANY FRIENDS', which is perhaps what feels like the most synergistic fusion between Quadeca's hip hop and experimental indie folk sides... the piano becomes almost neurotic, getting increasingly flooded by haunting vocal harmonies and generating this slightly anxious vibe that complements the song's themes of “struggling to keep up with friends” incredibly well." And mentions "DUSTCUTTER" and "UNDER MY SKIN" as "I Didn't Mean To Haunt You-style songs." This page identifies "Scrapyard" as Quadeca's. It also discusses genre fusion ("hip hop and experimental indie folk sides") and mentions themes like "struggling to keep up with friends" and "loneliness."

